WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.734 --> 00:00:02.202
- The other thing too,

00:00:02.202 --> 00:00:04.804 align:start size:94% position:6%
we should look at why
didn't the Red Wave come?

00:00:04.804 --> 00:00:07.007
And we should be very
honest with ourselves.

00:00:07.007 --> 00:00:08.208 align:start size:84% position:16%
There's a couple
different reasons why?

00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:09.042
And I'll tell you.

00:00:09.042 --> 00:00:10.777
Look, as leader,

00:00:10.777 --> 00:00:13.446 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm fortunate in
both terms we've won.

00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:17.417 align:start size:94% position:6%
But could we have won
more? Yeah, we could have.

00:00:17.417 --> 00:00:19.619 align:start size:84% position:16%
I had three candidates
in Pennsylvania

00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:23.089 align:start size:88% position:13%
that overperformed
the Republican
gubernatorial candidate

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:26.292
by 2014 and 11 and did not win.

00:00:26.292 --> 00:00:30.430
This idea that we're
just gonna pick whoever

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:31.965 align:start size:84% position:16%
and think we're gonna
win in November.

00:00:31.965 --> 00:00:33.433
No.

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:35.201
The top of the ticket's gonna
hurt you below the ticket.

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:36.836
And this idea that we're,

00:00:36.836 --> 00:00:38.638
it almost happens
that we're better

00:00:38.638 --> 00:00:40.340
at attacking other Republicans

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:42.842
than standing up for the
policies we believe in.

00:00:42.842 --> 00:00:45.545
(cheerful music)

00:00:49.983 --> 00:00:50.950
- Hey, guys.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:52.419
Let's give a very nice welcome

00:00:52.419 --> 00:00:56.623 align:start size:97% position:3%
to the Speaker of the House.
(audience applauding)

00:01:01.094 --> 00:01:03.463
- We did a press
conference in here

00:01:03.463 --> 00:01:06.032
and it wasn't like you'd see
before like the members get up.

00:01:06.032 --> 00:01:08.468
And we invited parents and kids.

00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:12.072
And we invited parents that
had the challenges, right,

00:01:12.072 --> 00:01:13.573
one was, you'll remember him,

00:01:13.573 --> 00:01:15.775
the father from Loudoun
County that his daughter,

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:17.077
- Oh, yeah.

00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:18.711
- He comes and tells his story.

00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:21.981
But then you have a young
mother from Rhode Island.

00:01:21.981 --> 00:01:25.151
Her child went to kindergarten

00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:27.454
and all she wanted to know,
what's on the curriculum?

00:01:27.454 --> 00:01:28.688
They said, "Well,
you gotta ask us.

00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:30.190
You gotta put it
in written form."

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:31.925
She goes, "Well, okay. Well,
then I had 100 questions."

00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:34.661 align:start size:81% position:19%
Then they had a
meeting to sue her

00:01:34.661 --> 00:01:35.728
for she was asking questions.

00:01:35.728 --> 00:01:37.464
It was unbelievable.

00:01:37.464 --> 00:01:40.300 align:start size:84% position:16%
And you think at times
these stories that

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.902
they just happened in one
little place, you know.

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:45.772
You hear about that male
swimmer against the women.

00:01:45.772 --> 00:01:47.974
Well, now when you start
having the discussion,

00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:49.843
it's happening everywhere.

00:01:49.843 --> 00:01:52.712
And so, we like to
bring more people in.

00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:54.681
And the other thing we're doing,

00:01:54.681 --> 00:01:57.150
taking our hearings
out of Washington.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.385
We've had them along the border,

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:01.754 align:start size:97% position:3%
we've had them in different
parts of the country.

00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:06.059
Make the committees go out
to listen to the public.

00:02:06.059 --> 00:02:07.527 align:start size:84% position:16%
- You can make some
noise for that.

00:02:07.527 --> 00:02:12.398 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yeah, yeah. There you go.
(audience applauding)

00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:14.801
I also like the way
we started that,

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:16.069
I didn't even have
to ask a question.

00:02:16.069 --> 00:02:17.604
You could just talk,
(audience laughing)

00:02:17.604 --> 00:02:19.339
and I'll just sit here with
my legs crossed for an hour

00:02:19.339 --> 00:02:20.607
and we'll make it work.

00:02:20.607 --> 00:02:21.975
- We've been interviewed before.

00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:23.243
- Yeah.

00:02:23.243 --> 00:02:24.911 align:start size:84% position:16%
- He's like most
people in California,

00:02:24.911 --> 00:02:25.745
he used to live there.

00:02:25.745 --> 00:02:27.614
Now he moved to Florida.

00:02:27.614 --> 00:02:30.683
- But I'd go down to his
house and we'd do a show.

00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:32.452
- Well, let's start actually,

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:35.221
since you talked about
the parental rights vote,

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:39.092 align:start size:94% position:6%
which did pass, but not
one Democrat voted for it.

00:02:39.092 --> 00:02:41.928
Five Republicans
voted against it.

00:02:43.096 --> 00:02:45.231 align:start size:84% position:16%
Transparency, that's
all it was about.

00:02:45.231 --> 00:02:47.200
And I find when I
talk to these guys,

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.035
I listen to my audience

00:02:49.035 --> 00:02:51.337
that's what most people want
right now out of government,

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:53.706
whether it's the schools
or whether it's here in DC.

00:02:53.706 --> 00:02:56.142
Just show us what's going
on. Be honest with us.

00:02:56.142 --> 00:02:58.044
Don't treat us like children.

00:02:58.044 --> 00:03:01.080
Are you shocked that not one
Democrat could vote for it?

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:03.650 align:start size:84% position:16%
And because I
can't go, you know,

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:07.720
more than a minute
without talking about
AOC, unfortunately,

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:10.356
that in her speech where
she was railing against it,

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:12.258
she called it fascism.

00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:13.760
She called it fascism.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:15.395
I take it, nothing's probably
surprises you at this point.

00:03:15.395 --> 00:03:16.963 align:start size:84% position:16%
- No. You know
what's interesting?

00:03:16.963 --> 00:03:18.698 align:start size:88% position:13%
And this is what people
have to understand,

00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:21.067
and I've come to understand
now with these Democrats,

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:23.303 align:start size:84% position:16%
they're very extreme
in their beliefs.

00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:25.605
I mean, think about,

00:03:25.605 --> 00:03:28.474
in Congress, when you put
a bill across the desk,

00:03:28.474 --> 00:03:30.343 align:start size:94% position:6%
H.R is the House
Resolution number, right?

00:03:30.343 --> 00:03:32.178 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it gets stamped by
the order in which you go.

00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:35.748 align:start size:84% position:16%
But the majority
reserves 1 through 10

00:03:35.748 --> 00:03:39.085
and the Democrats get
11 through 20, okay.

00:03:39.085 --> 00:03:40.787 align:start size:84% position:16%
So you can pick
what's most important.

00:03:40.787 --> 00:03:44.023 align:start size:88% position:13%
Now, our number one
bill is gonna be H.R.1,

00:03:44.023 --> 00:03:45.458
which we'll vote on this week,

00:03:45.458 --> 00:03:47.827 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that'll be for energy
independence, okay,

00:03:47.827 --> 00:03:49.429
the Democrats were
in the majority.

00:03:49.429 --> 00:03:52.498 align:start size:88% position:13%
Their number one bill
was election law reform.

00:03:52.498 --> 00:03:53.833
That they wanted
to rig the system.

00:03:53.833 --> 00:03:55.468 align:start size:84% position:16%
That will make
taxpayers pay for it,

00:03:55.468 --> 00:03:57.003
and have a Czar and all that.

00:03:57.003 --> 00:03:59.939
We made the parents
Bill of Rights H.R.5

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:01.441
for a couple different reasons.

00:04:01.441 --> 00:04:04.844
One, that's when kindergarten
starts here, five years old.

00:04:04.844 --> 00:04:07.413
But it really had five pillars.

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:10.516
Parents should know what's
being taught in the school.

00:04:10.516 --> 00:04:11.584
Give us the curriculum,

00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:12.986
not make us go have to find it.

00:04:12.986 --> 00:04:14.187
Send it to us.

00:04:14.187 --> 00:04:15.622 align:start size:81% position:19%
And what books
are there to read?

00:04:15.622 --> 00:04:16.723
They say, we're banning books.

00:04:16.723 --> 00:04:17.957
We never said anything about it.

00:04:17.957 --> 00:04:19.592
Just tell us what you
want us to read, right?

00:04:19.592 --> 00:04:21.127
Shouldn't we know?

00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:22.862
The next thing we sought,

00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:25.832
we should know how do
you spend the money?

00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:28.301
It's taxpayer money. What's
it being spent on in schools?

00:04:28.301 --> 00:04:30.770
Is it going just
to administration?

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:32.505
Where's it going?

00:04:32.505 --> 00:04:34.874
We wanna protect our
children's privacy.

00:04:34.874 --> 00:04:37.644
Are they selling
the data, right?

00:04:37.644 --> 00:04:40.680 align:start size:88% position:13%
We wanna know, is there
a violent activity?

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.549
'Cause what will happen is, a
child may be hurt on a campus,

00:04:43.549 --> 00:04:45.685 align:start size:88% position:13%
but you are only telling
those two children.

00:04:45.685 --> 00:04:48.888
That father, what
happened to him,

00:04:48.888 --> 00:04:50.123
he went and told.

00:04:50.123 --> 00:04:53.459
But the person who that
molested his daughter

00:04:53.459 --> 00:04:55.395
had already done it before.

00:04:55.395 --> 00:04:59.065 align:start size:97% position:3%
And the last part is, a parent
has a right to be heard.

00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:01.668
You should be able to go
to a school board meeting

00:05:01.668 --> 00:05:04.270
and not say you're a terrorist.

00:05:05.538 --> 00:05:08.007
I'm a big conservative
federalist and all that.

00:05:08.007 --> 00:05:09.208
That's not getting
in a school board.

00:05:09.208 --> 00:05:11.411 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's just respect
in the process,

00:05:11.411 --> 00:05:13.346
and that's just transparency.

00:05:13.346 --> 00:05:16.382
I'm very big on transparency.

00:05:16.382 --> 00:05:19.385
The Democrats try to
attack me all the time

00:05:19.385 --> 00:05:21.087
when I provide information,

00:05:21.087 --> 00:05:24.090 align:start size:88% position:13%
when they tried to only
show certain parts.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:26.326
Well, let the public
make that decision.

00:05:26.326 --> 00:05:27.727
That's the difference, I think.

00:05:27.727 --> 00:05:30.463
When we had this dis
discussion before,

00:05:30.463 --> 00:05:33.399
I was born into a democratic
family, but never a Democrat.

00:05:33.399 --> 00:05:36.002
So, I really think my
philosophy and beliefs

00:05:36.002 --> 00:05:38.638
are stronger than others
because I chose to reject

00:05:38.638 --> 00:05:41.174 align:start size:84% position:16%
what I heard for
something different.

00:05:41.174 --> 00:05:44.711
And what I believe in a
philosophy, I trust you.

00:05:44.711 --> 00:05:46.346
I'm gonna give you
the information,

00:05:46.346 --> 00:05:48.214
but I also trust you
to fail or succeed.

00:05:48.214 --> 00:05:50.717
I'm not gonna predetermine that.

00:05:50.717 --> 00:05:52.919
- So, I don't wanna
do just policy here

00:05:52.919 --> 00:05:54.420
because I'm guessing,

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:56.289
how many of you, is this
the first time either in,

00:05:56.289 --> 00:05:57.490
well, in the Capitol itself?

00:05:57.490 --> 00:05:59.192
I assume many of you, right?

00:05:59.192 --> 00:06:00.493 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Fabulous.
- Yeah.

00:06:00.493 --> 00:06:02.962
So, how weird is it on
just a day-to-day basis

00:06:02.962 --> 00:06:05.298
that you're in this
building right now,

00:06:05.298 --> 00:06:06.766 align:start size:94% position:6%
where pretty much
everyone's here right now

00:06:06.766 --> 00:06:08.234
for the most part, right,

00:06:08.234 --> 00:06:10.303 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you're in this
building with these people

00:06:10.303 --> 00:06:13.473
that the ideas are just
so diametrically opposed

00:06:13.473 --> 00:06:14.974
with each other.

00:06:14.974 --> 00:06:16.409
And you know, they say some
pretty nasty things about you

00:06:16.409 --> 00:06:17.944 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you gotta say
some things about them

00:06:17.944 --> 00:06:20.413
and sometimes they're on your
side of the aisle doing that.

00:06:20.413 --> 00:06:21.781
- Yeah. No, no.

00:06:21.781 --> 00:06:23.683
Look, if you follow
the Speaker's race,

00:06:23.683 --> 00:06:25.718 align:start size:84% position:16%
most people win on
the first round, okay.

00:06:25.718 --> 00:06:27.420
(audience laughing)
It's kind of a shock.

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:30.089
- He had a rough week.
He had a rough week.

00:06:30.089 --> 00:06:34.327 align:start size:97% position:3%
- But you know what? I'm Irish
and Italian, so it's okay.

00:06:34.327 --> 00:06:36.195
I have the best fights in
my neighborhood, right.

00:06:36.195 --> 00:06:38.564 align:start size:88% position:13%
And most fights go 15
rounds. So, that's okay.

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:40.199
But I'll tell you a story.

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:43.035
So, if you watch
the Speaker's race,

00:06:43.035 --> 00:06:44.570 align:start size:88% position:13%
there's this one staffer
who sat next to me,

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:45.905 align:start size:88% position:13%
John LeGansky, and
everybody wants to know.

00:06:45.905 --> 00:06:48.341 align:start size:88% position:13%
He's a really smart kid.
He went to Stanford.

00:06:48.341 --> 00:06:50.910 align:start size:88% position:13%
He was the youngest
guy ever run the floor.

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.780
And his brother's about to
become an ordained priest.

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:56.849
And his brother sent him a
book of prayers, you know,

00:06:56.849 --> 00:06:58.017
before it all.

00:06:58.017 --> 00:07:01.220 align:start size:88% position:13%
And he went and we
would keep a tally sheet

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:02.855 align:start size:84% position:16%
of everybody's name
that he'd keep voting.

00:07:02.855 --> 00:07:05.124 align:start size:84% position:16%
And before the
speaker votes, right,

00:07:05.124 --> 00:07:07.794 align:start size:84% position:16%
he just put a
handful down there.

00:07:07.794 --> 00:07:09.896
And when the Speaker
Race was over, he said,

00:07:09.896 --> 00:07:11.964
"You know what?

00:07:11.964 --> 00:07:13.399 align:start size:84% position:16%
We always knew it
was gonna go to 15."

00:07:13.399 --> 00:07:14.867
I said, "Well, how
did we always know?"

00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:17.336 align:start size:97% position:3%
He goes, "You know, I didn't
get a chance to finish

00:07:17.336 --> 00:07:19.272 align:start size:97% position:3%
all the prayers in the book
and this weekend I did.

00:07:19.272 --> 00:07:21.007 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, how many
prayers were in that book?

00:07:21.007 --> 00:07:22.575
15 prayers."

00:07:22.575 --> 00:07:24.610
He goes, "You know
how many ballots

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:26.145 align:start size:88% position:13%
I just grabbed a handful
of put down there?

00:07:26.145 --> 00:07:27.580
15."

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:29.782
So sometimes God
has a plan for you,

00:07:29.782 --> 00:07:31.818 align:start size:84% position:16%
you just don't
know it yet, right.

00:07:31.818 --> 00:07:33.486
I could have been
Speaker before.

00:07:33.486 --> 00:07:35.354
We're in the majority. It
was gonna be handed to me.

00:07:35.354 --> 00:07:38.724 align:start size:84% position:16%
But I made a mistake
on a interview.

00:07:38.724 --> 00:07:40.426
And lo and behold, pull back.

00:07:40.426 --> 00:07:42.261
We went back into the minority.

00:07:42.261 --> 00:07:43.629 align:start size:81% position:19%
And an interesting
thing happened.

00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:44.864
So, four years ago,

00:07:44.864 --> 00:07:47.600
I became leader of
the Republican Party

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:49.569
and Pelosi became Speaker.

00:07:49.569 --> 00:07:51.370
We have a five seat majority.

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:55.141
And those four years, we
picked up six more seats.

00:07:55.141 --> 00:07:58.110
Six Democrats lost
their congressional
seats in California.

00:07:58.110 --> 00:07:59.512
No one writes about this.

00:07:59.512 --> 00:08:00.847 align:start size:84% position:16%
She lost her majority
in her home state

00:08:00.847 --> 00:08:03.916
that people think is
really blue, right?

00:08:03.916 --> 00:08:06.519
But we were able to win.

00:08:06.519 --> 00:08:08.654
If anyone ever wants
to run for office,

00:08:08.654 --> 00:08:09.922
this is what I would tell you.

00:08:09.922 --> 00:08:12.592
And how long should the
members stay in office?

00:08:12.592 --> 00:08:15.127
If you don't get goosebumps
looking at that Capitol,

00:08:15.127 --> 00:08:18.064
walking into this building,
it's time for you to leave.

00:08:18.064 --> 00:08:19.565
Right?

00:08:19.565 --> 00:08:22.001
I feel so privileged to even
have this opportunity, right.

00:08:22.001 --> 00:08:24.103
I get goosebumps now just
talking to you about it.

00:08:24.103 --> 00:08:25.371
Think about it.

00:08:25.371 --> 00:08:26.439
We're sitting in the
Rayburn Room, right?

00:08:26.439 --> 00:08:29.275
The chambers is right there.

00:08:29.275 --> 00:08:32.778 align:start size:84% position:16%
Those chambers were
opened in 1857, right?

00:08:34.614 --> 00:08:37.483 align:start size:97% position:3%
George Washington was
never sworn in here, right?

00:08:37.483 --> 00:08:39.085
George Washington was
sworn in on Wall Street

00:08:39.085 --> 00:08:41.621
and in Philadelphia, okay.

00:08:41.621 --> 00:08:43.890 align:start size:88% position:13%
Every President since
has either been sworn in

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:46.692 align:start size:84% position:16%
on the east side,
over on the west side.

00:08:46.692 --> 00:08:49.228
The first President be sworn
on the west side was Reagan.

00:08:49.228 --> 00:08:52.131 align:start position:0%
He'd tell you he wanted to
look towards California, right?

00:08:52.131 --> 00:08:55.434
But my favorite place
in this whole building,

00:08:55.434 --> 00:08:56.769
if you go and take a tour,

00:08:56.769 --> 00:08:59.605 align:start size:88% position:13%
is that room right
there at Statuary Hall.

00:08:59.605 --> 00:09:01.541 align:start size:84% position:16%
So when the building
was first built,

00:09:01.541 --> 00:09:02.942
the country wasn't 50 states,

00:09:02.942 --> 00:09:05.211
it wasn't as large
people had desks.

00:09:05.211 --> 00:09:06.579
This dome is the second dome.

00:09:06.579 --> 00:09:08.381
They had to enlarge the dome

00:09:08.381 --> 00:09:09.882
when they enlarged the building.

00:09:09.882 --> 00:09:12.385
Like the Supreme Court was
over on the Senate side.

00:09:12.385 --> 00:09:14.253
So that was the chambers.

00:09:14.253 --> 00:09:16.155
And it's really a nice floor.

00:09:16.155 --> 00:09:17.156
It wasn't thin.

00:09:17.156 --> 00:09:18.524
It was platoons,
people spinning.

00:09:18.524 --> 00:09:19.792
People had fights,

00:09:19.792 --> 00:09:23.729
'cause this was before
the Civil War, right.

00:09:23.729 --> 00:09:26.933
But they put a tile with anyone

00:09:26.933 --> 00:09:29.969 align:start position:0%
who ever been a member of
Congress that became President.

00:09:29.969 --> 00:09:31.904
So you could see where they sat.

00:09:31.904 --> 00:09:35.408 align:start position:0%
So if you go all the way in
the back, there's a tile there.

00:09:35.408 --> 00:09:37.343
But like Father Sierra,

00:09:39.145 --> 00:09:40.179
there's a tile in front of it.

00:09:40.179 --> 00:09:41.714
It's where Abraham Lincoln sat.

00:09:41.714 --> 00:09:45.151 align:start size:97% position:3%
He was a one term congressman.
That's all he served.

00:09:45.151 --> 00:09:46.819
And so he sat in the back.

00:09:46.819 --> 00:09:49.422
And he was kind of a quiet man.

00:09:51.757 --> 00:09:52.858 align:start size:84% position:16%
And if you go in the
back, back there,

00:09:52.858 --> 00:09:54.160
they had like, a male room,

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:55.361
and we call it the Lincoln Room.

00:09:55.361 --> 00:09:57.296
He'd go back there
and tell his stories.

00:09:57.296 --> 00:09:59.632 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I like to stand
right where that tile is.

00:09:59.632 --> 00:10:02.134
Stand there and look front,
like at the front of it.

00:10:02.134 --> 00:10:04.136
Envision it being
loud, you know,

00:10:04.136 --> 00:10:05.671
They didn't have
microphones on it.

00:10:05.671 --> 00:10:09.809
And then look back and look
at the lady above the clock.

00:10:09.809 --> 00:10:12.979 align:start size:97% position:3%
She's supposed to be
inscribing what happens there.

00:10:12.979 --> 00:10:14.380
And just look at the clock

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:17.216
and pause and look
at the time, right?

00:10:17.216 --> 00:10:19.919
Look at the time.
Say, oh, it's 2:30.

00:10:19.919 --> 00:10:21.687
And you wanna know why
that's my favorite spot.

00:10:21.687 --> 00:10:26.092 align:start size:97% position:3%
That's the same clock, the
same view Abraham Lincoln had.

00:10:26.092 --> 00:10:27.026
And I'll promise you,

00:10:27.026 --> 00:10:28.694
he's looked at it many times

00:10:28.694 --> 00:10:31.831 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you're standing
right where he is, right.

00:10:31.831 --> 00:10:35.301 align:start size:84% position:16%
Runs for Senate loses,
runs for President.

00:10:35.301 --> 00:10:36.268
And this is one of the things

00:10:36.268 --> 00:10:39.472
that drives me as a Republican.

00:10:39.472 --> 00:10:42.008 align:start size:84% position:16%
He's the first
Republican President.

00:10:42.008 --> 00:10:45.311
Here's a nation not even know
if it's gonna sustain itself.

00:10:45.311 --> 00:10:47.613 align:start size:94% position:6%
He builds the
Intercontinental railroad.

00:10:47.613 --> 00:10:50.416 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's like inventing
the internet, right,
during the time.

00:10:50.416 --> 00:10:53.185
That he's looking at the
future's gonna be brighter.

00:10:53.185 --> 00:10:57.156
But this is what I think
all Americans should pause.

00:10:57.156 --> 00:10:58.724
Did you see that poll in
the Wall Street Journal

00:10:58.724 --> 00:11:00.559 align:start size:81% position:19%
where we're not
patriotic anymore.

00:11:00.559 --> 00:11:02.962 align:start size:97% position:3%
We don't want to have
children. We're not religious.

00:11:02.962 --> 00:11:04.397
That concerns me.

00:11:05.264 --> 00:11:07.667
And what I would say is,

00:11:07.667 --> 00:11:09.969
look at Lincoln's words, right.

00:11:09.969 --> 00:11:11.771
And what I believe if Lincoln,

00:11:11.771 --> 00:11:14.807 align:start size:97% position:3%
if he was interviewing Lincoln
today and Lincoln was here,

00:11:14.807 --> 00:11:16.242
Lincoln would first
take off his top hat.

00:11:16.242 --> 00:11:19.211
He'd be a tall man.
He has a high voice.

00:11:19.211 --> 00:11:20.446
But he would tell you

00:11:20.446 --> 00:11:22.048
about the "Gettysburg
Address," you know.

00:11:22.048 --> 00:11:23.349
"Fourscore and seven years ago,

00:11:23.349 --> 00:11:24.650
our forefathers brought forth

00:11:24.650 --> 00:11:27.286
on this continent, a
new nation," right,

00:11:27.286 --> 00:11:29.622 align:start size:84% position:16%
"conceived in
liberty and dedicated

00:11:29.622 --> 00:11:32.058 align:start size:84% position:16%
to the proposition
that we're all equal."

00:11:32.058 --> 00:11:34.860
He goes on to say, "But if we
fail government of the people,

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:39.865
by the people, for the people,
shall not perish from earth."

00:11:41.367 --> 00:11:43.536
But think about when he wrote
this and why he said it, okay.

00:11:43.536 --> 00:11:45.237
We're not the world power.

00:11:45.237 --> 00:11:47.406 align:start size:88% position:13%
We're not even a
country. We're divided.

00:11:47.406 --> 00:11:49.408
But he knew America's
more than a country.

00:11:49.408 --> 00:11:51.977 align:start size:88% position:13%
An idea that we would
still sustain ourselves,

00:11:51.977 --> 00:11:55.881
the concept of by the
people, for the people

00:11:55.881 --> 00:11:58.551
is more powerful
than anything else.

00:11:58.551 --> 00:12:00.953
But name me one other nation
that's conceived in liberty

00:12:00.953 --> 00:12:03.989 align:start size:97% position:3%
and dedicated the proposition
that we're all equal.

00:12:03.989 --> 00:12:05.224
We're not a perfect nation.

00:12:05.224 --> 00:12:07.393 align:start size:84% position:16%
We strive to be a
more perfect nation,

00:12:07.393 --> 00:12:10.863
but we should be more patriotic.

00:12:10.863 --> 00:12:12.331
I mean, this idea,

00:12:12.331 --> 00:12:15.367 align:start size:97% position:3%
and this it goes long answer
to your short question.

00:12:15.367 --> 00:12:16.569
I will put bills on the floor.

00:12:16.569 --> 00:12:17.470 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I don't even
remember the question.

00:12:17.470 --> 00:12:18.938
(audience laughing)

00:12:18.938 --> 00:12:21.006
- I just now, Dave.

00:12:21.006 --> 00:12:22.241
- I think I asked
you what it's like

00:12:22.241 --> 00:12:24.443
to work in a place when
people don't like you.

00:12:24.443 --> 00:12:25.544
- Okay.

00:12:25.544 --> 00:12:28.681 align:start size:84% position:16%
So we had a resolution
on the floor

00:12:28.681 --> 00:12:31.383
denouncing socialism, okay.

00:12:31.383 --> 00:12:34.754 align:start size:97% position:3%
This is not a college
campus. This is not Berkeley.

00:12:34.754 --> 00:12:37.523
This is people elected to the
US House of Representatives

00:12:37.523 --> 00:12:39.458
of all the history of America

00:12:39.458 --> 00:12:42.495
and 100 Democrats
couldn't vote, no.

00:12:42.495 --> 00:12:44.497
Can you believe that?

00:12:44.497 --> 00:12:45.965
So it goes back
to your question,

00:12:45.965 --> 00:12:49.668 align:start size:97% position:3%
could I believe that not
one Democrat would stand up?

00:12:49.668 --> 00:12:51.537
That's an extreme position.

00:12:51.537 --> 00:12:54.140
And I say this with
all due respect.

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:56.408
The Democrats where I grew
up, they were not that way.

00:12:56.408 --> 00:12:57.376
- [Dave] Yeah.

00:12:57.376 --> 00:12:58.577
- You would find places here.

00:12:58.577 --> 00:13:00.646
And look, we have passed,

00:13:00.646 --> 00:13:03.549 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'll get this wrong,
in the last week,

00:13:03.549 --> 00:13:07.386
we're somewhere about
43 or 48 bills, okay?

00:13:07.386 --> 00:13:11.123 align:start size:94% position:6%
Over like 37 of them
have all been bipartisan.

00:13:11.123 --> 00:13:15.461
There's been more than 4,000
Democrat votes on these, right?

00:13:15.461 --> 00:13:19.565 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so, you can find common
ground with both sides.

00:13:19.565 --> 00:13:23.102
But how can it not be common
ground that parents can know

00:13:23.102 --> 00:13:25.237 align:start size:84% position:16%
what's being taught
in their kid's school?

00:13:25.237 --> 00:13:27.840
No way do they get to
say what the curriculum

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:30.009
or what the books
are being read.

00:13:30.009 --> 00:13:31.977
Just tell us what you're doing.

00:13:31.977 --> 00:13:33.312
And that's wrong?

00:13:33.312 --> 00:13:34.847
- So how do we get over that?

00:13:34.847 --> 00:13:36.982
Because I think that that
seems to be probably the,

00:13:36.982 --> 00:13:39.285
there's a lot of problems but
that seems to be the first one

00:13:39.285 --> 00:13:41.120 align:start size:84% position:16%
that when you look
back and you go, okay,

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:42.555
well these guys
aren't, you know,

00:13:42.555 --> 00:13:45.324
they are afraid to
denounce socialism.

00:13:45.324 --> 00:13:47.359
Many of them do view the
founding of the country

00:13:47.359 --> 00:13:50.663 align:start size:97% position:3%
as a net negative to the
world, things of that nature.

00:13:50.663 --> 00:13:51.897
How do we get past that

00:13:51.897 --> 00:13:53.032 align:start size:81% position:19%
before we get into
the battles of,

00:13:53.032 --> 00:13:54.667
okay, you want this tax break

00:13:54.667 --> 00:13:56.468
and you want that spending bill?

00:13:56.468 --> 00:13:59.605
- Well it's tough too, 'cause
it's just like on DC Crime.

00:13:59.605 --> 00:14:02.408 align:start size:88% position:13%
Now, all of you stay
safe here, but you know,

00:14:02.408 --> 00:14:03.342
DC is not a State.

00:14:03.342 --> 00:14:05.144
They wanna make it a state.

00:14:05.144 --> 00:14:09.448
And the city council went and
decriminalized carjacking,

00:14:09.448 --> 00:14:11.517
even forms of murder.

00:14:11.517 --> 00:14:13.419 align:start size:84% position:16%
So we could do
something about it.

00:14:13.419 --> 00:14:17.489
We put it on the floor to
not allow that to happen.

00:14:17.489 --> 00:14:18.724
One member on the
Democratic side

00:14:18.724 --> 00:14:20.392
got mugged on her way to work

00:14:20.392 --> 00:14:22.294
and she lives in a
really nice area.

00:14:22.294 --> 00:14:24.296
But it's not unusual, right?

00:14:24.296 --> 00:14:26.031
You talk to staff, it
happens all the time.

00:14:26.031 --> 00:14:27.333
- Rand Paul's, one
of his staff members,

00:14:27.333 --> 00:14:29.501 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Got stabbed.
- was stabbed two days ago.

00:14:29.501 --> 00:14:30.769 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think he's in
critical condition.

00:14:30.769 --> 00:14:32.171
- The other weekend before,

00:14:32.171 --> 00:14:34.540
in broad daylight in the
middle of the weekend,

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:38.143 align:start size:84% position:16%
had a gunpoint
robbed and the phone

00:14:38.143 --> 00:14:39.645
and everything else taken.

00:14:39.645 --> 00:14:42.381
Carjackings, we did
this in February.

00:14:42.381 --> 00:14:43.849
At that time there were,

00:14:43.849 --> 00:14:45.751
we had only about 30 some
days into the new year

00:14:45.751 --> 00:14:47.920
and there were 65 carjackings.

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:51.190 align:start position:0%
When we passed this, the
President said he would veto it.

00:14:51.190 --> 00:14:53.592
Only 31 Democrats voted with us.

00:14:53.592 --> 00:14:56.462 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's not a partisan
issue to be safe.

00:14:56.462 --> 00:14:57.997
- So, what is it
you hear from them?

00:14:57.997 --> 00:14:59.798
What do you hear privately
when they vote against it?

00:14:59.798 --> 00:15:01.600 align:start size:84% position:16%
- They think they
have to lock together.

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:04.937
That somehow, oh, that I'm
agreeing with the Republican.

00:15:04.937 --> 00:15:07.506 align:start size:88% position:13%
No, you want your
streets safe, you know.

00:15:07.506 --> 00:15:10.843
And they said, "But
DC should be a state

00:15:10.843 --> 00:15:12.177 align:start size:81% position:19%
and we should have
our hands off."

00:15:12.177 --> 00:15:14.914
Now, the delegate
for DC, Norton,

00:15:14.914 --> 00:15:18.784
she did a rally before it
was gonna come back, right?

00:15:18.784 --> 00:15:20.386
And she had it at Union Station

00:15:20.386 --> 00:15:22.354
in the middle of the
day at three o'clock.

00:15:22.354 --> 00:15:23.956
And if you watch the
video of the rally,

00:15:23.956 --> 00:15:25.658 align:start size:88% position:13%
somebody gets carjacked
during the rally.

00:15:25.658 --> 00:15:28.427 align:start size:88% position:13%
(audience laughing)
You can't make this up.

00:15:28.427 --> 00:15:30.195
You can't make it up.

00:15:31.897 --> 00:15:33.933
But this is the other thing,
and this is what scares me.

00:15:33.933 --> 00:15:35.968
So, it wasn't just about DC,

00:15:35.968 --> 00:15:39.171
it's about what's happening
across this nation in crime

00:15:39.171 --> 00:15:40.739
from Chicago and others.

00:15:40.739 --> 00:15:42.241
And I believe there
are some waking up,

00:15:42.241 --> 00:15:43.475
but the majority are not

00:15:43.475 --> 00:15:45.044
'cause they think they
can't stand up to them.

00:15:45.044 --> 00:15:48.280 align:start size:84% position:16%
DC also says,
"Anybody can vote."

00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:49.949
Okay, we're in pretty much

00:15:49.949 --> 00:15:51.951
an international
city, are we not?

00:15:51.951 --> 00:15:53.385
'cause you have all
these embassies.

00:15:53.385 --> 00:15:56.221
So you can belong to the
Chinese Communist Party,

00:15:56.221 --> 00:15:58.357
be a Chinese and work
at that embassy in DC

00:15:58.357 --> 00:16:01.427
now said, you can vote
in your elections here.

00:16:01.427 --> 00:16:02.795
What do you think they
would wanna vote for

00:16:02.795 --> 00:16:05.965
and embarrass our
nation's Capitol.

00:16:05.965 --> 00:16:07.800
That's what concerns me.

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:09.201
That's beyond common sense.

00:16:09.201 --> 00:16:11.870
That's an extreme position

00:16:11.870 --> 00:16:14.106 align:start size:94% position:6%
that goes beyond the
foundation of the nation.

00:16:14.106 --> 00:16:16.342 align:start position:0%
- So what do you think
Republicans can do better then?

00:16:16.342 --> 00:16:17.943
One of the lines that
these guys hear me say

00:16:17.943 --> 00:16:20.245
probably five times a week is,

00:16:20.245 --> 00:16:21.680
you don't have to
be a Republican,

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:23.449
but you cannot be a Democrat.

00:16:23.449 --> 00:16:25.351 align:start size:84% position:16%
And the reason I
frame it that way is,

00:16:25.351 --> 00:16:27.353
it's obvious they've,
for the most part,

00:16:27.353 --> 00:16:28.821
gone off the deep end.

00:16:28.821 --> 00:16:30.956
But there clearly is some
sort of branding issue

00:16:30.956 --> 00:16:33.258 align:start size:88% position:13%
or something that I
think might be changing.

00:16:33.258 --> 00:16:34.760
I do believe that,

00:16:34.760 --> 00:16:36.528
that, you know, for example,
the Republicans obviously,

00:16:36.528 --> 00:16:38.664
didn't get that Red
Wave in the midterm.

00:16:38.664 --> 00:16:41.300 align:start size:97% position:3%
So people clearly are stepping
away from the Democrats,

00:16:41.300 --> 00:16:44.403
but they're kind of, ah, I
don't know these Republicans,

00:16:44.403 --> 00:16:45.871
- You know, the other thing too,

00:16:45.871 --> 00:16:49.641 align:start size:94% position:6%
we should look at why
didn't the Red Wave come?

00:16:49.641 --> 00:16:52.011
And we should be very
honest with ourselves.

00:16:52.011 --> 00:16:53.912 align:start position:0%
There's a couple different
reasons why, and I'll tell you.

00:16:53.912 --> 00:16:58.417 align:start size:97% position:3%
Look, as leader, I'm fortunate
in both terms we've won.

00:16:58.417 --> 00:17:02.321 align:start size:94% position:6%
But could we have won
more? Yeah, we could have.

00:17:02.321 --> 00:17:05.257 align:start position:0%
I had three candidates in
Pennsylvania that overperformed

00:17:05.257 --> 00:17:07.893 align:start size:88% position:13%
the Republican
gubernatorial candidate

00:17:07.893 --> 00:17:11.196
by 2014 and 11 and did not win.

00:17:11.196 --> 00:17:15.000
This idea that we're
just gonna pick whoever

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:16.769 align:start size:84% position:16%
and think we're gonna
win in November.

00:17:16.769 --> 00:17:18.237
No.

00:17:18.237 --> 00:17:20.039
The top of the ticket's gonna
hurt you below the ticket.

00:17:20.039 --> 00:17:21.673
And this idea that we're,

00:17:21.673 --> 00:17:23.475
it almost happens
that we're better

00:17:23.475 --> 00:17:25.077
at attacking other Republicans

00:17:25.077 --> 00:17:27.980
than standing up for the
policies we believe in.

00:17:27.980 --> 00:17:30.382
Well, that gets us nowhere.

00:17:30.382 --> 00:17:32.117
And the other thing too is,

00:17:32.117 --> 00:17:33.652
if you wanna be in the majority,

00:17:33.652 --> 00:17:35.154
and this is what Reagan said,

00:17:35.154 --> 00:17:37.589
"If you agree with us 80%
of the time, you're there."

00:17:37.589 --> 00:17:39.024
Somebody who wins in Texas

00:17:39.024 --> 00:17:41.693 align:start position:0%
is gonna be different than
someone who wins in California.

00:17:41.693 --> 00:17:44.063
And if you wanna sit back and
where did we win the majority?

00:17:44.063 --> 00:17:46.965
We woN five new seats in New
York, we won in California,

00:17:46.965 --> 00:17:50.069
we won in Oregon,
we won in Arizona,

00:17:50.069 --> 00:17:51.703
places that other
Republicans lost.

00:17:51.703 --> 00:17:54.073
And to be frank, in
the last two cycles,

00:17:54.073 --> 00:17:55.674
we've lost the presidency.

00:17:55.674 --> 00:17:58.210
Both cycles lost in the Senate.

00:17:58.210 --> 00:17:59.478
We've lost Governors.

00:17:59.478 --> 00:18:01.046
The only place we've
won is in the House.

00:18:01.046 --> 00:18:03.215 align:start size:97% position:3%
And the one thing I will
say, the determining factor,

00:18:03.215 --> 00:18:04.716
the quality of the candidate.

00:18:04.716 --> 00:18:07.653
We have an extraordinary
candidates who've won.

00:18:07.653 --> 00:18:09.755 align:start size:81% position:19%
And sometimes it's
very important

00:18:09.755 --> 00:18:12.224
to get the right person
through the primary.

00:18:12.224 --> 00:18:13.692
And you shouldn't just say,

00:18:13.692 --> 00:18:17.162
okay, well this person
is the most conservative.

00:18:17.162 --> 00:18:19.965 align:start size:84% position:16%
Well, who's the most
conservative who
can win in November?

00:18:19.965 --> 00:18:21.200 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's another
thing we have think,

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:23.202
because you gotta have
the gavel to govern.

00:18:23.202 --> 00:18:26.004 align:start size:94% position:6%
The uniqueness, like we
have a five seat majority.

00:18:26.004 --> 00:18:28.006
They don't hand the gavels out
in small, medium, and large.

00:18:28.006 --> 00:18:31.043 align:start size:84% position:16%
We get the same
size gavel, and so.

00:18:31.043 --> 00:18:32.878 align:start size:97% position:3%
And this is a place as
Republicans should wake up.

00:18:32.878 --> 00:18:36.281
So I look back at
the vote saying,

00:18:36.281 --> 00:18:38.617
where could we have done better?

00:18:38.617 --> 00:18:40.552
Top of the ticket hurt.

00:18:41.820 --> 00:18:43.856
Supreme Court decision
made a difference too,

00:18:43.856 --> 00:18:46.058
in different places where
the Democrats played it.

00:18:46.058 --> 00:18:47.526
It did.

00:18:47.526 --> 00:18:49.862
- So you think it hurt more
than anticipated, maybe?

00:18:49.862 --> 00:18:51.630 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, much more than
the polling showed.

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:55.100 align:start size:84% position:16%
It determined turnout
and motivation.

00:18:55.100 --> 00:18:57.336
In 2012, we had 234 members

00:18:58.804 --> 00:19:00.806
and we didn't even win the
popular vote in the House.

00:19:00.806 --> 00:19:02.808
In 2016, we peaked.

00:19:02.808 --> 00:19:07.646
We had 242 members and we won
the popular vote by one point.

00:19:07.646 --> 00:19:10.048
Today we have 222 members,

00:19:10.048 --> 00:19:11.650
you know what our
popular vote was?

00:19:11.650 --> 00:19:13.318
We won it by three points.

00:19:13.318 --> 00:19:15.521 align:start size:84% position:16%
Two points better
than the time before.

00:19:15.521 --> 00:19:19.725
Seven points better than
we did two years prior.

00:19:19.725 --> 00:19:21.760
So what it means is,
it's more competitive.

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:23.595
We've had redistricting.

00:19:23.595 --> 00:19:25.430 align:start size:97% position:3%
So, it's not that you're gonna
have these big majorities

00:19:25.430 --> 00:19:27.799
that you think things
are just gonna happen.

00:19:27.799 --> 00:19:31.470
And people look through
different points, right?

00:19:31.470 --> 00:19:33.105
I mean, think for a moment,

00:19:33.105 --> 00:19:35.340
how many are you
from Pennsylvania?

00:19:35.340 --> 00:19:36.742
The one thing I learned is,

00:19:36.742 --> 00:19:39.311
Democrats will vote for whoever

00:19:39.311 --> 00:19:40.546
as long as they're a Democrat.

00:19:40.546 --> 00:19:43.015
(audience laughing)

00:19:43.015 --> 00:19:44.149
You know?

00:19:44.149 --> 00:19:45.584
But I will tell you,

00:19:45.584 --> 00:19:46.818 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Where is John Fetterman?
(audience laughing)

00:19:46.818 --> 00:19:47.886
Where is he?

00:19:47.886 --> 00:19:48.887
Where is he?

00:19:49.721 --> 00:19:51.356
- I don't know.

00:19:51.356 --> 00:19:52.858
But I'm just, you know,

00:19:52.858 --> 00:19:56.929
all due respect, I mean,
it's just very difficult.

00:19:56.929 --> 00:19:58.597
But Republicans,

00:19:58.597 --> 00:20:00.599
and this is where I say
we've gotta be honest

00:20:00.599 --> 00:20:01.867 align:start size:81% position:19%
with ourselves
and we gotta do it

00:20:01.867 --> 00:20:03.969
before you get to election,

00:20:03.969 --> 00:20:06.805 align:start size:94% position:6%
if we have a poor
candidate, rightfully so,

00:20:06.805 --> 00:20:08.607
we won't all walk and lock step

00:20:08.607 --> 00:20:11.176 align:start size:84% position:16%
and vote for that
person, rightfully so.

00:20:11.176 --> 00:20:13.979 align:start size:88% position:13%
So what we really should
do in the primary,

00:20:13.979 --> 00:20:15.547
not beat up on each other,

00:20:15.547 --> 00:20:16.848
but pick the very best person

00:20:16.848 --> 00:20:19.151
who's gonna be able
to win and carry it.

00:20:19.151 --> 00:20:21.019
And sometimes we
say, if you're not,

00:20:21.019 --> 00:20:23.355 align:start size:84% position:16%
no one's gonna be
100% with each other.

00:20:23.355 --> 00:20:25.958 align:start size:97% position:3%
But do we have a core
foundation with one another?

00:20:25.958 --> 00:20:27.593 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I'll tell you guys something
funny about John Fetterman.

00:20:27.593 --> 00:20:30.329
I actually saw Dr.
Oz a couple weeks ago

00:20:30.329 --> 00:20:31.997 align:start size:84% position:16%
at Jordan Peterson's
house at a party.

00:20:31.997 --> 00:20:35.234 align:start size:88% position:13%
And he looked like he
got punched in the face.

00:20:35.234 --> 00:20:39.471 align:start size:94% position:6%
The guy cannot believe
that he lost to Fetterman.

00:20:39.471 --> 00:20:41.506 align:start size:84% position:16%
It is still,
(audience chattering)

00:20:41.506 --> 00:20:42.307
Any who.

00:20:43.375 --> 00:20:45.010
What do you make
of, as you know,

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:46.545
I lived in Cali,

00:20:46.545 --> 00:20:48.780
I made a very public exit to
the free State of Florida.

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:50.082
Things seem to be,
- I'm not giving up.

00:20:50.082 --> 00:20:51.783
I'm not giving up.
(audience laughing)

00:20:51.783 --> 00:20:53.185
- You know, I didn't
wanna leave that house,

00:20:53.185 --> 00:20:54.453
but that was the only thing

00:20:54.453 --> 00:20:55.754
that was keeping me
there was a home.

00:20:55.754 --> 00:20:57.256
- I didn't wanna
leave that house,

00:20:57.256 --> 00:21:00.626 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I couldn't afford to
buy that house there either.

00:21:00.626 --> 00:21:02.561
- But what do you make
of the general idea now

00:21:02.561 --> 00:21:05.530
that the states do seem to
be going their separate way

00:21:05.530 --> 00:21:08.267 align:start size:88% position:13%
and that is what
Federalism's all about.

00:21:08.267 --> 00:21:10.002
I mean, I feel like I
moved to a new country,

00:21:10.002 --> 00:21:10.936
not a new state.

00:21:10.936 --> 00:21:11.803
- Each state is a pilot program.

00:21:11.803 --> 00:21:13.305
- Yeah.

00:21:13.305 --> 00:21:14.239 align:start size:97% position:3%
- And if there's one thing,
you look in the nation, right,

00:21:14.239 --> 00:21:16.341
every 10 years we do a census.

00:21:16.341 --> 00:21:19.611
This is the first time in
the history of California

00:21:19.611 --> 00:21:21.613 align:start size:84% position:16%
that we lost a
Congressional seat,

00:21:21.613 --> 00:21:23.215
the time before we stayed equal.

00:21:23.215 --> 00:21:24.182
And what states lost?

00:21:24.182 --> 00:21:25.984
New York, California, Illinois.

00:21:25.984 --> 00:21:27.286
What states gained?

00:21:27.286 --> 00:21:29.554
Texas and Florida.

00:21:29.554 --> 00:21:32.591
It's a pilot program that's
telling the country, right.

00:21:32.591 --> 00:21:35.560
And how many, you have
friends that have moved?

00:21:35.560 --> 00:21:37.629
Why? Because of the policies.

00:21:37.629 --> 00:21:42.534 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it should wake the
other states up to change.

00:21:42.534 --> 00:21:44.336
- I mean, do you have
any hope for Cali?

00:21:44.336 --> 00:21:46.938 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean obviously you're still
a representative in Cali.

00:21:46.938 --> 00:21:48.440
I mean, do you have hope

00:21:48.440 --> 00:21:50.008 align:start size:94% position:6%
that that thing can move.
- Yeah, because look,

00:21:50.008 --> 00:21:52.511 align:start size:97% position:3%
I've won five seats in the
last four years in California,

00:21:52.511 --> 00:21:55.113
Republican and Congress.

00:21:55.113 --> 00:21:56.481
They make the system hard there.

00:21:56.481 --> 00:21:57.749
The other thing you
have to remember too,

00:21:57.749 --> 00:22:00.752
California has, when it
comes to election law,

00:22:00.752 --> 00:22:01.753
they mail everybody a ballot.

00:22:01.753 --> 00:22:03.488
Whether you ask for one or not.

00:22:03.488 --> 00:22:05.023
They don't clean up their rules.

00:22:05.023 --> 00:22:07.793
They make it legal that you can

00:22:07.793 --> 00:22:10.929 align:start size:88% position:13%
harvest somebody's
ballot and we still win.

00:22:10.929 --> 00:22:13.398
So it doesn't matter
what state you live in,

00:22:13.398 --> 00:22:14.900
find what the rules are

00:22:14.900 --> 00:22:17.169 align:start size:88% position:13%
and go out and fight
for what you believe in.

00:22:17.169 --> 00:22:18.403
The hard part is,

00:22:18.403 --> 00:22:20.238
most people who are
leaving California

00:22:20.238 --> 00:22:21.940
have a more conservative view.

00:22:21.940 --> 00:22:23.742
And you know, these
other states say,

00:22:23.742 --> 00:22:25.177
"Oh, we don't want the Cali."

00:22:25.177 --> 00:22:26.745
I want them back, you know.

00:22:26.745 --> 00:22:28.280
They're a lot of
the other voters.

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:29.748
But we're learning.

00:22:29.748 --> 00:22:31.183
And also the other thing too is,

00:22:31.183 --> 00:22:35.253 align:start size:97% position:3%
if you look at the Republican
Party now in the House,

00:22:35.253 --> 00:22:36.922
more women, more minorities,

00:22:36.922 --> 00:22:39.958 align:start size:94% position:6%
the most Black Republicans
since reconstruction.

00:22:39.958 --> 00:22:43.228 align:start position:0%
So don't think we can't compete
anywhere in the country.

00:22:43.228 --> 00:22:44.196
We can.

00:22:44.196 --> 00:22:46.064
But I believe it's the policy.

00:22:46.064 --> 00:22:49.201 align:start position:0%
And so, I don't give up because
the pendulum always swings.

00:22:49.201 --> 00:22:50.869
- How much do you view
the mainstream media

00:22:50.869 --> 00:22:53.372 align:start position:0%
as an impediment to what you're
trying to do at this point?

00:22:53.372 --> 00:22:56.274 align:start size:97% position:3%
They obviously are losing
control to a certain degree.

00:22:56.274 --> 00:22:57.509 align:start size:84% position:16%
But, you know, every
day on the show,

00:22:57.509 --> 00:22:59.544
I'm putting out the
lies of The View

00:22:59.544 --> 00:23:01.513
and the lies of Meet The Press

00:23:01.513 --> 00:23:05.550 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the lies of CNN and
MSNBC and the whole thing.

00:23:05.550 --> 00:23:07.052
They're never with you guys.

00:23:07.052 --> 00:23:10.422
They don't mind lying over
and over and over again.

00:23:10.422 --> 00:23:11.923
Do you view it as
a problem still

00:23:11.923 --> 00:23:15.527
or in some ways is it a win
because we can expose it now?

00:23:15.527 --> 00:23:18.063 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, I think
the American public

00:23:18.063 --> 00:23:19.965
on both sides of the aisle

00:23:19.965 --> 00:23:22.768 align:start size:97% position:3%
do not trust what they're
being told most of the time.

00:23:22.768 --> 00:23:24.770 align:start size:84% position:16%
'Cause they found
that it hasn't been,

00:23:24.770 --> 00:23:26.705 align:start size:94% position:6%
there's lots of times
that it hasn't been right.

00:23:26.705 --> 00:23:29.040
I live it each and every day.

00:23:29.040 --> 00:23:31.510
I can walk up and do
a press conference,

00:23:31.510 --> 00:23:34.179
I'll tell you on
right after passing

00:23:34.179 --> 00:23:35.881
the Parents' Bill of Rights

00:23:35.881 --> 00:23:39.117
and they'll ask me something
about something else, right.

00:23:39.117 --> 00:23:41.653
But to tell you the secret is,

00:23:41.653 --> 00:23:44.256
I kind of enjoy the battle now.

00:23:44.256 --> 00:23:46.858 align:start size:97% position:3%
I don't know if you watch
any of my press conferences?

00:23:46.858 --> 00:23:49.428
I decided when I
became a speaker,

00:23:49.428 --> 00:23:50.695 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm gonna do
something different.

00:23:50.695 --> 00:23:52.964
And I do a lot of
things different.

00:23:52.964 --> 00:23:54.433 align:start size:84% position:16%
Hakeem Jeffries'
office is right over,

00:23:54.433 --> 00:23:55.967
that used to be my office.

00:23:55.967 --> 00:23:59.704 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I treat Hakeem the way
I wanted to be treated.

00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:03.141
We should be the first
ones that respect people

00:24:03.141 --> 00:24:04.910
who have difference
of opinion of us.

00:24:04.910 --> 00:24:06.611
Because we get upset
if people don't give us

00:24:06.611 --> 00:24:08.480
the respect of our opinions.

00:24:08.480 --> 00:24:10.449 align:start size:94% position:6%
Well, how can you do that
if you don't respect?

00:24:10.449 --> 00:24:12.851
So, I try to provide that.

00:24:12.851 --> 00:24:14.252
But I don't go down

00:24:14.252 --> 00:24:15.520 align:start size:94% position:6%
and do a press conference
in the Press Room.

00:24:15.520 --> 00:24:17.489
I do it in the Statutory
Hall, I do it out here.

00:24:17.489 --> 00:24:19.090
And they all come around me

00:24:19.090 --> 00:24:22.661
and they'll ask me
this one question.

00:24:22.661 --> 00:24:26.765
When I was dealing from the
beginning about on intel.

00:24:26.765 --> 00:24:29.534
I removed Schiff and Swalwell.

00:24:29.534 --> 00:24:33.939
I did it for a purpose, not
for a political purpose.

00:24:33.939 --> 00:24:36.308
And this one reporter
asked me the question

00:24:36.308 --> 00:24:37.709
and then soon as
I start to answer,

00:24:37.709 --> 00:24:39.411 align:start size:88% position:13%
she says, I'm not
answering the question.

00:24:39.411 --> 00:24:40.645
So my point is,

00:24:40.645 --> 00:24:42.747
well, you have a right
to ask me the question,

00:24:42.747 --> 00:24:43.982 align:start size:84% position:16%
but you do not
have a right to say

00:24:43.982 --> 00:24:45.283
whether I answered or not.

00:24:45.283 --> 00:24:47.118
My answer is your answer.

00:24:47.118 --> 00:24:48.887
But I mean, think about it.

00:24:48.887 --> 00:24:50.388
I was really concerned about

00:24:50.388 --> 00:24:52.757 align:start size:97% position:3%
Intel committee's different
than all the other committees.

00:24:52.757 --> 00:24:56.394
This is where you get the
security clearance, okay.

00:24:56.394 --> 00:24:57.662
The members who sit on there

00:24:57.662 --> 00:25:00.432
only get appointed
by their leaders.

00:25:02.601 --> 00:25:05.470
They find things out that
other members don't, right.

00:25:05.470 --> 00:25:08.139
And we make decisions in
there to protect all of us

00:25:08.139 --> 00:25:09.341
with information we know.

00:25:09.341 --> 00:25:10.575 align:start size:81% position:19%
And because of
source and others,

00:25:10.575 --> 00:25:12.644
you really can't let
other people know.

00:25:12.644 --> 00:25:14.112
Well, Schiff used that position

00:25:14.112 --> 00:25:15.914
to lie about what he knew,

00:25:15.914 --> 00:25:18.350
which you can't be there.

00:25:18.350 --> 00:25:20.986 align:start size:97% position:3%
Swalwell got on as a sophomore
and the minute he gets on,

00:25:20.986 --> 00:25:22.187
the FBI comes tells us,

00:25:22.187 --> 00:25:24.656 align:start size:88% position:13%
he's got a relationship
with a Chinese spy.

00:25:24.656 --> 00:25:25.891
No disrespect.

00:25:25.891 --> 00:25:27.626
I just don't think that
qualifies you, right.

00:25:27.626 --> 00:25:29.928
(audience laughing)

00:25:29.928 --> 00:25:31.463
- No disrespect. No disrespect.

00:25:31.463 --> 00:25:32.597 align:start size:84% position:16%
(audience laughing)
- Yeah but,

00:25:32.597 --> 00:25:33.798
there's 200 other members

00:25:33.798 --> 00:25:35.333 align:start size:97% position:3%
she could appointed, right?
- Yeah.

00:25:35.333 --> 00:25:36.935
- I never knew about this
until I came later there.

00:25:36.935 --> 00:25:38.203
And I'm reading the papers.

00:25:38.203 --> 00:25:39.971
I had the FBI come and
give me the briefing.

00:25:39.971 --> 00:25:41.172 align:start size:88% position:13%
There's no way he should
have been on there.

00:25:41.172 --> 00:25:42.841
And he was kept on there.

00:25:42.841 --> 00:25:44.843
And so I just said, "No.

00:25:46.177 --> 00:25:47.579 align:start size:94% position:6%
Anybody that has a
relationship with any spy,

00:25:47.579 --> 00:25:48.880
you can't be on the committee."

00:25:48.880 --> 00:25:50.181
(audience laughing)

00:25:50.181 --> 00:25:52.350
I mean, it's not a high bar.

00:25:52.350 --> 00:25:53.618
(audience laughing)

00:25:53.618 --> 00:25:55.387
- Wait, I have to
pause you for a sec

00:25:55.387 --> 00:25:58.056
'cause when I had you
at the house in Cali,

00:25:58.056 --> 00:25:59.824 align:start size:84% position:16%
you told me about that
for the first time,

00:25:59.824 --> 00:26:01.760 align:start size:84% position:16%
it was the first time
I heard about it.

00:26:01.760 --> 00:26:03.361
And we then clipped
that a million times

00:26:03.361 --> 00:26:04.729
and it got millions of views

00:26:04.729 --> 00:26:06.164
and people were sharing it

00:26:06.164 --> 00:26:09.267
before you were Speaker and now.

00:26:09.267 --> 00:26:12.137
But he's still here. He's
still in this building.

00:26:12.137 --> 00:26:14.172 align:start size:94% position:6%
If you have a relationship
with the Chinese spy,

00:26:14.172 --> 00:26:15.807
no disrespect,

00:26:15.807 --> 00:26:16.775
I just became very Italian.

00:26:16.775 --> 00:26:17.976
Do you see that?

00:26:17.976 --> 00:26:20.478 align:start size:84% position:16%
Isn't that just like,
you gotta go now?

00:26:20.478 --> 00:26:21.980
Like you can't stay here.

00:26:21.980 --> 00:26:23.248
You don't have to go home
but you can't stay here,

00:26:23.248 --> 00:26:25.850
- I believe in the rule of law.

00:26:25.850 --> 00:26:29.120 align:start size:97% position:3%
The voters decide who their
member of Congress is.

00:26:29.120 --> 00:26:30.555
I could dislike that person.

00:26:30.555 --> 00:26:32.223 align:start size:94% position:6%
I could think that person
did something wrong.

00:26:32.223 --> 00:26:35.660
But there's nothing
that disqualifies him

00:26:36.962 --> 00:26:38.229
from being a member.

00:26:38.229 --> 00:26:41.499
He's a member and I
give him committees,

00:26:41.499 --> 00:26:44.769
but I'm not giving you
a security clearance.

00:26:44.769 --> 00:26:46.905
If he was to leave this job

00:26:46.905 --> 00:26:48.373 align:start size:81% position:19%
and he went to the
private sector,

00:26:48.373 --> 00:26:49.908
he could not get a
security clearance

00:26:49.908 --> 00:26:52.611 align:start size:97% position:3%
because he had a relationship
with a Chinese spy.

00:26:52.611 --> 00:26:55.013 align:start size:97% position:3%
So why would we give him a
government security clearance?

00:26:55.013 --> 00:26:56.581
And they attacked before it.

00:26:56.581 --> 00:26:58.249
And so what I did,

00:26:58.249 --> 00:27:01.820
I went down when the intel
committee first constituted

00:27:01.820 --> 00:27:03.521
and I had Hakeem come with me.

00:27:03.521 --> 00:27:04.789
And I told all the members,

00:27:04.789 --> 00:27:06.257
"Look, I want you
to work together.

00:27:06.257 --> 00:27:08.760 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't want partisanship
in this office

00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:10.829 align:start size:97% position:3%
because this is a
committee that should think

00:27:10.829 --> 00:27:13.031
just as Americans, right.

00:27:13.031 --> 00:27:14.699 align:start size:84% position:16%
And this is the other
thing I'm doing.

00:27:14.699 --> 00:27:16.901
I'm really afraid,

00:27:16.901 --> 00:27:21.206
which country captures AI
and quantum first, okay.

00:27:21.206 --> 00:27:24.476
And we should be really
concerned as Americans

00:27:24.476 --> 00:27:25.710
of what's happening

00:27:25.710 --> 00:27:28.947
with Russia, China,
North Korea and Iran.

00:27:30.348 --> 00:27:33.518
If you study history, this
is very similar to the 1930s.

00:27:33.518 --> 00:27:36.588
They're creating
an axis of power

00:27:36.588 --> 00:27:39.991 align:start size:94% position:6%
of supporting one
another against the West,

00:27:39.991 --> 00:27:42.661
democracy and freedom.

00:27:42.661 --> 00:27:44.863
The only two times President Xi

00:27:44.863 --> 00:27:48.233
has left his country
since the pandemic

00:27:48.233 --> 00:27:51.136
was to meet with this group.

00:27:51.136 --> 00:27:52.771
It's very concerning to me.

00:27:52.771 --> 00:27:56.541 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so MIT teaches a
course in quantum and AI

00:27:58.009 --> 00:28:00.745
that they actually provide to
the generals in our military.

00:28:00.745 --> 00:28:04.215 align:start size:97% position:3%
